WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.325 --> 00:00:03.200
Are they going to be
very personal questions?

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:03.700
Yeah.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:04.440
Deep.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:05.360
Like hard hitting.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.640
So you're Brazilian.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.140
Yeah.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:10.834
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:10.834 --> 00:00:14.934
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.820
Can I just start this,
taking this out of the way?

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:23.510
Knowing you was one of the best
things that happened to me.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:25.000
I truly love you, Lizzie.

00:00:25.001 --> 00:00:25.550
You, too.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.550
And I want everybody
here to know that.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:29.250
I feel the same way.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:33.500
And I think this connection
sort of formed from day one

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.820
and then got deeper.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.980
I will never forget
the day that we were

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:38.937
doing this scene in the car.

00:00:38.937 --> 00:00:40.520
- Yes!
- We really didn't know 
each other well.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.860
But, you know, and then we
were stuck in this car.

00:00:42.861 --> 00:00:43.560
Yeah.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:44.790
And we were driving.

00:00:44.791 --> 00:00:44.990
Yeah.

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.970
There was a crew in
another car shooting us.

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:49.500
And then they said, make a left.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:50.510
[LAUGHS]
Yeah. 

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:51.642
And then we made a left.

00:00:51.642 --> 00:00:53.600
Neither of us know how
to drive around Chicago.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:54.910
And then we ended
up in the freeway.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:55.910
[LAUGHS]
Yes. 

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:58.038
And the radio stopped working.

00:00:58.038 --> 00:00:59.330
And I didn't have a cell phone.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.510
And it was like, and I was
a Brazilian dude driving--

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:03.500
This old car.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.580
This old car with no
connection with anyone.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:06.080
Nope.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.580
Basically, we were
like literally--

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:08.820
We were on our way to Indiana.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.820
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.150
Yeah, we did contemplate
just continuing to drive--

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:13.660
To drive.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.627
To Indiana, just
never going back.

00:01:15.627 --> 00:01:16.835
And I think that after that--

00:01:16.835 --> 00:01:17.570
That was it.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:18.440
That was the moment.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.120
That was--

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.200
I was like, I called the AD.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.770
And I was like, "I don't
know where we are."

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:27.102
"Can you please tell
us how to come back?"

00:01:27.102 --> 00:01:28.310
Come rescue us.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:30.060
What is the show about?

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.059
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.970
For me, the show is about
this women struggling

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.450
to regain her power in
a very tricky situation

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:42.750
because the guy who attacked her
is not a regular serial killer.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.238
He's a dude that travels--

00:01:44.238 --> 00:01:44.780
Through time.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.342
Through time.

00:01:46.342 --> 00:01:49.010
And it's like, in
order to do that, she

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:52.190
has the help of this journalist,
this guy that I play,

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:55.190
who is a very complicated
man, and who is also

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:58.430
trying to regain his power.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.790
One of the things that I think
that connects Kirby and Dan

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:05.390
is that they are both very
vulnerable human beings

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:08.270
in a very specific
moment of their lives.

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:09.770
And also sort of
hiding something

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:10.530
about themselves, right? 

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.530
Hiding a truth 
about themselves

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.678
that they feel like they
can't expose to anybody else.

00:02:15.678 --> 00:02:17.970
In Kirby's case, it's the
trauma that she went through.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.180
And it's the fact that
her whole world shifts.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:24.380
For me in the beginning,
Kirby is a means for me

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.360
to get my big story
about it, which

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:28.430
is my comeback into journalism.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.270
But then there is
something about this woman.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.140
When things change for
you, do you recognize it?

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.880
Sometimes.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.255
Other times,
they're just random.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:41.820
This is a question
that I have for you.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.520
You're always so cool in a set.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.210
Do you ever get nervous?

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:49.290
I don't know if I get
nervous that much anymore.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:50.730
Emotional stuff, I'm fine.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.320
That doesn't make me nervous.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:53.070
That I can do.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.070
I know how to do that.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.910
But if I've got a scene
with a lot of dialogue,

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:59.310
I get anxious, or tense.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.350
The scene at the back
of the Bee Happy Bar.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:04.380
Things change for
me just like that.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.473
And nobody knows any different.

00:03:06.473 --> 00:03:07.890
Not only was it a
lot of dialogue,

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.390
but it was a lot
of tricky dialogue.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:12.660
Having to figure out how to
explain the shifts to you

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:14.640
and really wanting
the scene to work,

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.560
and really wanting it
to work as a director.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.890
So that's where I
get a little anxious.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.900
Lizzie, I was always like
watching you direct

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:25.050
and acting at the same
time, which is something

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:26.910
that you have done
before, especially

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.460
coming from doing a
very strong scene,

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.200
and things were going on.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.685
And then at some
point, you said, "cut".

00:03:33.685 --> 00:03:34.480
Very sweet.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.200
And I'm like, how does
that work for you?

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.900
I've always thought
in terms of editing.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.290
I've always been really aware
of what shots were going

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.270
to be used for which moments.

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.100
There are times
when I definitely

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.110
have to remind myself
not to be the director,

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:51.690
and to just be the
actor, either when

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.148
it's a really important 
performance moment

00:03:53.148 --> 00:03:55.740
for my character, or when
it's an important performance

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.330
moment for the other character.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:02.310
What I love about directing
you is learning how far I could

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.760
push you and how
far you could go,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.360
and knowing that
you were totally

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.210
happy to be pushed
to go all the way,

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.290
practically off of the cliff.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:15.778
This is him.

00:04:15.778 --> 00:04:17.070
I don't know how to explain it.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:18.209
But this is him.

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:19.890
No, there will be none of that.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:20.479
This is him.

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:21.440
This is him.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:22.310
This is him!

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:25.470
We're not doing any of that.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.780
What are the kind of stories
that you want to tell?

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.150
How did you come
across Shining Girls?

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:33.420
For me, I wanted to do a show
that wasn't like something

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:34.308
I had seen before.

00:04:34.308 --> 00:04:35.850
What I loved about
this is I've never

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:37.950
done anything that
was as magical.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.740
I've never done anything that
had any sort of time travel

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.590
element to it and yet
was really grounded.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:46.560
The mix of genres, I
think that's very exciting

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:47.910
and kind of unique.

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:49.980
It was never you.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:51.013
It's this house.

00:04:51.013 --> 00:04:52.680
I feel like I should
ask you a question.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:53.547
You're asking me all
kinds of questions.

00:04:53.548 --> 00:04:54.500
I'm a journalist.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.205
Tell me about-- cause I 
remember--

00:04:57.205 --> 00:04:59.550
no, but you do love journalism.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:00.120
I do.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:03.660
I think that journalism is a
very, very important thing.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.930
And I really, really
respect journalists

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:11.750
that keep doing their thing
despite of anything else.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:12.250
Yeah.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.880
You are still here.

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.800
And no one could forget you.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:21.130
This show has something that's
very interesting and connected

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:24.850
to the world nowadays,
which is women empowerment.

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:25.540
Yeah.

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:28.930
It's about the woman that
survived and struggled

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:33.280
to regain her power,
and also to not allow

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.050
that to happen to other women.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.810
I think that's what
Dan and Kirby connect

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:41.500
about, is they want to tell
the women who didn't make it,

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:42.370
who didn't survive.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:43.870
And they want to
tell their stories.

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:47.500
And they don't want them
to just be forgotten.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:50.980
You think the world
owes you, and that you

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:53.410
can take whatever you want.

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:55.820
But this is mine.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:06:00.580
This was a truly great,
amazing experience in my life.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.340
To be with you, to know
you, to become your friend

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:04.900
for me, the greatest thing.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:06.270
That's how I feel.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:06.770
I agree.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:08.050
And I agree the
greatest thing for me

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:09.790
and the experience
of making the show

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:11.620
has been the
relationship between

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:14.230
you and I that 
has developed.

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:18.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:18.771 --> 00:06:21.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:21.921 --> 00:06:23.720
[CYMBAL CRASHING]

